<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wagria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wagria is a historical region in eastern Holstein, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between the Bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a> and the Bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wagri">Wagrians</a>, a West Slavic people of the Obotrite confederation whose chief centre was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/starigard">Starigard</a> (Oldenburg). Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-ii-of-holstein">Adolf II of Holstein</a> conquered it in the 1130s and 1140s, settled it with Germans, Flemings and Frisians and founded Lübeck in 1143.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wagria.jpg" alt="Wagria" width="320" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joan Blaeu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_Wagrien.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and still on early modern maps, Wagria was bounded on the north and east by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> from the Kiel Fjord to Lübeck Bay, and inland by the rivers Schwentine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trave">Trave</a>. It corresponds roughly to the present districts of Plön and Ostholstein in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a>, though the name now usually means only the Oldenburg Peninsula, whose highest point is the Bungsberg at 168 metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wagri">Wagri</a>, a Slavic Lechitic tribe, and a region so called existed at least as early as the 8th century, covering the land between the Kiel Fjord and the middle and lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trave">Trave</a>. Its chief stronghold stood at the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/starigard">Starigard</a>, the Old Castle, now Oldenburg in Holstein, where the ramparts survive. Other important settlements were Old Lübeck, or Liubice, and Plön, or Plune.</p><p>The chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmold-of-bosau">Helmold of Bosau</a> gives a vivid contemporary account of how, in 1143, Count Adolf II of Schauenburg and Holstein brought settlers from Holstein and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stormarn">Stormarn</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> and Holland to make Wagria a profitable land, as part of the German eastward expansion of the High Middle Ages. According to Helmold, those from Holstein received the most sheltered land west of Segeberg on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trave">Trave</a>, the Westphalians the country around Dargun, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eutin">Eutin</a> and the Frisians Suesel, while the Plön area remained uninhabited. The count let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> settle Oldenburg, Lütjenburg and other coastal districts in return for taxes, so the local Slavs also took part in the new settlement of the region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wagria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successors pushed into Slavic <b>Wagria</b>, and Adolf II founded Lübeck in 1143.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holstein">County of Holstein</a></li></ul></section>
